    Avoid “canvas-only” pages

If a page (like page.immersive) is literally:

    Header/footer hidden,
    One <canvas> and a bunch of <div> shells with no content until JS runs,

Google may see it as “thin” or “empty”, especially in reduced JS rendering scenarios.

Your fix: ensure that even when JS is disabled, the HTML contains:

    A headline (<h1> / <h2>) describing the experience.
    A bit of copy about the brand, collections, and navigation.
    Fallback links to key categories.

2. Get your metadata right: titles, meta descriptions, OG tags

Dawn already provides a solid baseline for <title> and <meta name="description">. Review and adjust for your immersive homepage.

In layout/theme.liquid (or via Dawn’s snippets/meta-tags.liquid):

    Make sure:
        Home page title: includes your brand + primary keyword(s) (e.g. “Luxury Pakistani Designer Wear | Brand Name”).
        Home page meta description: 140–160 characters summarizing your offerings.

Shopify docs on this: SEO and meta tags in themes.

You don’t need anything special for the immersive JS here—just ensure:

    page.immersive has a meaningful page title and description in the Page resource in admin.
    Use {{ page.title }} / {{ page_description }} properly.

3. Structured data (JSON‑LD) for products and collections

Shopify recommends using JSON‑LD structured data for products, collections, and breadcrumbs:
JSON-LD structured data for SEO.

Dawn already includes structured data for products and blog posts. For your immersive experience:

    Ensure product pages (the canonical /products/{handle} pages) keep Dawn’s JSON‑LD intact.

    Your glass-product (sections/glass-product.liquid) endpoint is rendered via ?section_id=glass-product inside the glass panel. That content is for UX, not SEO. You don’t need separate structured data there, as long as:
        The canonical product page has correct JSON‑LD.
        You don’t mark the panel content as a separate page in structured data.

If you want to add organization or website structured data on the home page, you can add a small JSON‑LD snippet in theme.liquid or a home section.
4. Performance: crucial for SEO (Core Web Vitals)

Google explicitly uses performance metrics (LCP, CLS, INP) and best practices as ranking signals. The immersive WebGL experience is heavy by nature, so we want to contain the impact.

Shopify’s performance guide:
Performance best practices

You’re already doing some good things:

    Only load three.min.js, immersive-store.js, and immersive-theme.css on index + page.immersive.
    Use requestAnimationFrame and some optimization.

You can further harden SEO-impacting performance by:

    Ensuring above-the-fold content on mobile is fast

    On mobile, if the immersive canvas takes long to render, your Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) might be delayed.

    Consider:
        Slightly reducing the canvas resolution / devicePixelRatio on mobile (as discussed earlier).
        Keeping the top of the page simple and light (e.g. hero heading + CTA + initial fallback image) loading quickly, with canvas bootstrapping in the background.

    Use a static fallback image for very slow / no WebGL

You already have showWebGLFallback(canvas) which injects an <img> if WebGL is not supported.

SEO-wise, that’s helpful:

    If some bots or low-end devices don’t support WebGL, they will at least see a background image, plus your editorial sections + links.
    Good to keep that.

    Avoid blocking the main thread on load

    Three.js + your script should be defer loaded (Dawn’s script_tag is already defer in modern versions).
    Don’t do heavy synchronous work in module top-level; you’ve already wrapped most initialization in bindImmersiveInit() which is called after DOM is ready — that’s good.

5. Internal linking & crawl depth

A lot of merchants with immersive homepages accidentally hide their real catalog behind JS. For SEO you want:

    Clear anchor links to:
        Top categories (Eid, Bridal & Mehndi, Casual Pret, etc.).
        Popular brands (Suffuse, Soraya, Saad Bin Shahzad).
        Key landing pages (e.g., “Eid Collection 2026”).

Where to do it in your structure:

    In sections/immersive-editorial.liquid:
        Each editorial (Designer houses, Occasions, Featured collections) should include some static <a href="..."> links to important collections.

    In sections/immersive-product-grid.liquid:
        Use headings and descriptive intros, plus links to “View all {collection}” pages.

    In the footer or an auxiliary nav:
        Ensure a simple text sitemap-like structure of main collections is accessible even on the immersive homepage (unless you fully hide header/footer; if you do, consider a secondary compact nav).

6. Accessibility (SEO-adjacent but important)

Your immersive experience already uses:

    ARIA labels, roles, and focus management.
    aria-hidden properly on overlays and layers.

That’s excellent because:

    Accessibility improvements often correlate with better crawlability and rendering for bots as well.
    A clean, semantic DOM makes Google’s parser happier.

Keep:

    Using <h1>, <h2>, etc., in a logical hierarchy.
    Text labels for interactive elements (buttons, links) via aria-label or visible text.
    Avoiding text baked only into images.

7. Avoid SEO pitfalls with overlays/panels

Your glass panel and editorial overlay are client-side overlays. SEO gotchas:

    Don’t permanently change the URL when you open an overlay (e.g., using history.pushState('/products/...')) unless you handle canonical URLs carefully.
    You already fetch product HTML from /products/{handle}?section_id=glass-product but are not replacing the page URL. That’s good from an SEO point of view:
        The canonical product page remains /products/{handle}.
        The overlay is just a UX enhancement.

Just ensure:

    Your canonical tags on product pages remain untouched and correct.
    You don’t output canonical tags inside the glass-product section itself when fetched via AJAX; only in the full product template.

8. Content strategy: high-quality copy around your immersive feature

Search engines ultimately rank you based on:

    Intent match (what people search vs what your content covers),
    Content quality (depth, uniqueness, helpfulness),
    Topical authority.

Your immersive store is a presentation layer. For SEO, you still need:

    Strong collection descriptions:
        Unique, descriptive text about each collection (Eid, Luxury Formals, Bridal & Mehndi, Casual Pret, brand x’s line).
    Strong product descriptions:
        Unique, non-duplicated text (avoid copy-pasting manufacturer text across many sites).
    Supporting content:
        Blog posts / editorial sections (your immersive-editorial is great for this) that talk about styles, occasions, how to choose outfits, etc.

Make sure sections/immersive-editorial.liquid doesn’t just have visuals; give it:

    300–800 words of real, well-written content (broken into headings and paragraphs).
    Internal links to relevant collections/products.

9. Measuring SEO impact

Once you have the above in place:

    Use Google Search Console to:
        Check coverage (which URLs are indexed).
        See which pages get impressions/clicks.
        Spot any “Page indexed but blocked” or JS-rendering issues.

    Use PageSpeed Insights / Lighthouse for:
        LCP, CLS, INP on the home page and page.immersive.
        JavaScript execution time.

When you see performance or crawl issues, you can then optimize specific pieces (e.g. cut DPR, simplify shaders, or add more static content above the fold).
TL;DR: For your current setup

Given your Dawn-based immersive store:

    Ensure real HTML links and text exist on the home/immersive page:
        Use immersive-editorial.liquid to host rich, descriptive text + normal <a> links.
        Optionally add a simple fallback nav list for key collections/brands.

    Preserve / polish titles & meta descriptions:
        Use Shopify’s built-in meta handling, but optimize the strings in admin.

    Leave the WebGL as progressive enhancement:
        Fallback image already exists — keep it.
        Avoid full “canvas-only” pages with no textual content.

    Don’t duplicate structured data inside AJAX sections**:
        Let canonical product pages carry JSON‑LD.

    Watch performance:
        Lower pixel ratio slightly if needed.
        Avoid unnecessary DOM reads in animation loops (as we just optimized).